1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,687
Welcome to NAS Capacity Pulse, the Volume Anomaly Detection and Storage Forecaster.

2
00:00:04,687 --> 00:00:09,375
In most enterprises, storage monitoring relies heavily on tools like Grafana, which

3
00:00:09,375 --> 00:00:14,064
provides excellent server-level dashboards using Prometheus or InfluxDB metrics. However, Grafana reports

4
00:00:14,064 --> 00:00:18,751
disk usage only at the server or volume level. It cannot break

5
00:00:18,751 --> 00:00:23,439
down storage consumption by individual sub-applications, teams, or services running on that

6
00:00:23,439 --> 00:00:28,127
server. NAS Capacity Pulse solves this gap by providing sub-application level granularity,

7
00:00:28,128 --> 00:00:32,816
letting you see exactly which team or service is consuming the most

8
00:00:32,815 --> 00:00:37,503
space. The login screen provides secure authentication with username, password, and SSO

9
00:00:37,503 --> 00:00:42,192
support.

10
00:00:42,192 --> 00:00:46,560
The Dashboard provides a real-time overview of your entire storage infrastructure. Summary

11
00:00:46,560 --> 00:00:50,928
cards display total servers, sub-applications, space used, file count, and estimated monthly

12
00:00:50,927 --> 00:00:55,295
storage cost. The server filter at the top allows multi-select analysis. You

13
00:00:55,295 --> 00:00:59,664
can select one or more servers to dynamically update all charts and

14
00:00:59,664 --> 00:01:04,031
cards. Clicking Scan Now triggers an immediate refresh from the Linux agents.

15
00:01:04,032 --> 00:01:08,400
Unlike Grafana, which would show you only that a server has used,

16
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:12,768
say, sixty percent of its disk, NAS Capacity Pulse tells you that

17
00:01:12,768 --> 00:01:17,135
the billing-service used two point six gigabytes, the payment-gateway used one point

18
00:01:17,135 --> 00:01:21,503
seven gigabytes, and application-logs consumed one point six gigabytes on that same

19
00:01:21,504 --> 00:01:25,871
server.

20
00:01:25,871 --> 00:01:30,504
The pie chart visualizes space distribution across servers. The Sub-App Space Breakdown

21
00:01:30,504 --> 00:01:35,136
bar chart ranks all sub-applications by size, sorted in descending order so

22
00:01:35,135 --> 00:01:39,768
the largest consumers appear at the top. This is something Grafana simply

23
00:01:39,768 --> 00:01:44,400
cannot do, because Grafana relies on node-exporter metrics which only report filesystem-level

24
00:01:44,400 --> 00:01:49,032
usage. Below, the Growth, Purge, and Forecast chart shows the past one

25
00:01:49,031 --> 00:01:53,664
year and projects storage trends up to five years ahead. Notice the

26
00:01:53,664 --> 00:01:58,296
server dropdown in the top-right corner, allowing you to filter by individual

27
00:01:58,295 --> 00:02:02,927
servers or view all servers combined. The dark green line represents actual

28
00:02:02,927 --> 00:02:07,560
data growth at approximately twenty percent per year. The light blue dashed

29
00:02:07,560 --> 00:02:12,192
horizontal line marks the average volume across all historical data. The red

30
00:02:12,192 --> 00:02:16,824
line shows actual purge activity, with light red extending into the future

31
00:02:16,824 --> 00:02:21,456
for projected purge. Enterprises typically purge ten to fifteen percent annually, resulting

32
00:02:21,455 --> 00:02:26,087
in steady net growth that this chart clearly visualizes.

33
00:02:26,087 --> 00:02:30,866
Individual server cards display the IP address, mount path, and per sub-app

34
00:02:30,866 --> 00:02:35,645
breakdowns with file counts and directory counts. Each server runs a lightweight

35
00:02:35,645 --> 00:02:40,424
POSIX shell agent that uses only standard unix tools like find, du,

36
00:02:40,424 --> 00:02:45,203
stat, and curl. No Python or Java installation is required. The agent

37
00:02:45,204 --> 00:02:49,983
performs sixty-minute incremental scans, checking only for changes since the last scan,

38
00:02:49,983 --> 00:02:54,762
making it efficient even on servers with ten terabytes of data. Here

39
00:02:54,762 --> 00:02:59,540
you can see four servers including the newly added test-server with four

40
00:02:59,540 --> 00:03:04,319
sub-applications: web-frontend, api-gateway, data-pipeline, and log-aggregator.

41
00:03:04,319 --> 00:03:09,343
The Purge Eligibility Report helps identify old files for cleanup. You can

42
00:03:09,343 --> 00:03:14,367
filter by server, sub-application, and age thresholds from seven days to one

43
00:03:14,367 --> 00:03:19,391
year using quick-select buttons. After clicking Analyze, summary cards show total purge

44
00:03:19,391 --> 00:03:24,415
candidates and reclaimable space. The Purge History section displays eligible data across

45
00:03:24,415 --> 00:03:29,439
four time horizons: one week, one month, one year, and five years.

46
00:03:29,439 --> 00:03:34,463
Grafana offers no built-in concept of purge eligibility or file aging. In

47
00:03:34,463 --> 00:03:39,487
Grafana, you would need custom scripts and separate dashboards to identify which

48
00:03:39,487 --> 00:03:44,512
files can be safely removed. NAS Capacity Pulse integrates this natively with

49
00:03:44,512 --> 00:03:49,536
delete buttons that require explicit user confirmation before any file removal.

50
00:03:49,536 --> 00:03:54,135
Growth Predictions and Space Forecast provides both size and cost projections. Current

51
00:03:54,135 --> 00:03:58,735
usage summary shows total storage and monthly cost at your configured dollar

52
00:03:58,735 --> 00:04:03,335
rate. The forecast table projects growth over one week, one month, one

53
00:04:03,336 --> 00:04:07,936
year, and five years, with predicted monthly and period costs. The Purge

54
00:04:07,936 --> 00:04:12,536
Rate section shows daily purge potential and net storage after purging. In

55
00:04:12,536 --> 00:04:17,136
Grafana, you would typically use Prometheus prediction functions like predict-linear, but those

56
00:04:17,136 --> 00:04:21,736
only forecast at the disk level and have no cost modeling. NAS

57
00:04:21,735 --> 00:04:26,336
Capacity Pulse ties predictions directly to dollar amounts, helping teams plan storage

58
00:04:26,336 --> 00:04:30,936
budgets and anticipate capacity upgrades.

59
00:04:30,935 --> 00:04:35,723
File Extension Analytics is a unique feature not found in Grafana or

60
00:04:35,723 --> 00:04:40,512
any common monitoring tool. It identifies all unique file extensions and provides

61
00:04:40,512 --> 00:04:45,300
actionable optimization recommendations. For example: large dot-dat binary files should be moved

62
00:04:45,299 --> 00:04:50,087
to cold storage. Old dot-log files should use log rotation. Dot-wav and

63
00:04:50,087 --> 00:04:54,875
dot-bmp formats can be converted to compressed alternatives for significant space savings.

64
00:04:54,875 --> 00:04:59,663
Dot-bak and dot-tmp files older than seven days are typically safe to

65
00:04:59,663 --> 00:05:04,451
delete. Large CSV, XML, and JSON files can be compressed with gzip

66
00:05:04,451 --> 00:05:09,240
for sixty to ninety percent space reduction. These data-driven recommendations help teams

67
00:05:09,240 --> 00:05:14,028
reclaim space effectively. In Grafana, you would have no visibility into file

68
00:05:14,027 --> 00:05:18,815
types whatsoever.

69
00:05:18,815 --> 00:05:23,879
The Settings page provides centralized configuration management. Server Management lets administrators add

70
00:05:23,879 --> 00:05:28,944
or remove servers from the monitoring pool. Sub-application configurations define mount paths,

71
00:05:28,943 --> 00:05:34,007
file patterns, and folder structures for each team or service. Cost Estimation

72
00:05:34,007 --> 00:05:39,072
Settings control the dollar-per-gigabyte rate. Excluded Mount Paths ensure system directories like

73
00:05:39,072 --> 00:05:44,136
var, opt, and tmp are skipped. Capacity Planning sets daily consumption estimates,

74
00:05:44,135 --> 00:05:49,199
growth rates, purge schedules, and monthly allocations per team. When usage reaches

75
00:05:49,199 --> 00:05:54,264
eighty percent of allocated capacity, automated email alerts notify the responsible team

76
00:05:54,263 --> 00:05:59,327
contacts.

77
00:05:59,327 --> 00:06:04,302
To summarize, NAS Capacity Pulse stands apart from tools like Grafana with

78
00:06:04,302 --> 00:06:09,277
its sub-application level granularity. While Grafana monitors at the server or filesystem

79
00:06:09,277 --> 00:06:14,252
level, NAS Capacity Pulse drills down to individual services and teams. Additional

80
00:06:14,252 --> 00:06:19,227
differentiators include file extension deep-dive analytics, controlled purge workflow with user confirmation,

81
00:06:19,227 --> 00:06:24,202
cost forecasting tied to actual dollar amounts, a lightweight shell agent requiring

82
00:06:24,202 --> 00:06:29,177
no Python or Java, and capacity planning with automated threshold alerting. Thank

83
00:06:29,177 --> 00:06:34,151
you for watching this demonstration of NAS Capacity Pulse.

